scenarios.modelAnswer:
Three defects, in the order they bite.

**1. The interaction and the fake clock are not connected.** `user-event` schedules a `setTimeout` between keystrokes (its `delay`, default `0`). With fake timers installed and nothing advancing them, `await user.type(...)` is waiting on a timer that will never fire, which is the CI timeout. Testing Library says this explicitly and points at the `advanceTimers` option; `delay: null` is documented as *not* recommended because it removes the macrotask boundary the library relies on (https://testing-library.com/docs/using-fake-timers, https://testing-library.com/docs/user-event/options#advancetimers). The fix is `userEvent.setup({ advanceTimers: vi.advanceTimersByTime })`: the interaction now drives the same clock the assertion does.

**2. The clock is installed inside the test and torn down from somewhere else.** `vi.useFakeTimers()` sits in the body while only `afterEach` restores it, so any early failure leaves the fake clock installed for whatever runs next, and the failure becomes order-dependent. Install in `beforeEach`, and in `afterEach` flush what is pending before restoring — `vi.runOnlyPendingTimers(); vi.useRealTimers();` — because third-party code may have scheduled work that would otherwise be dropped when the real clock comes back.

**3. The assertion cannot fail for the reason it claims to test.** Asserting one call after advancing the full delay is satisfied by *any* implementation that eventually calls `onSearch`: a zero-delay debounce, a plain `onChange` handler, or a debounce that ignores its delay input all pass. There is no negative edge and no reset case, so the test certifies the call, not the debouncing.

The rewrite:

```ts
beforeEach(() => {
  vi.useFakeTimers();
});

afterEach(() => {
  vi.runOnlyPendingTimers();
  vi.useRealTimers();
});

it('calls onSearch once, only after the delay elapses', async () => {
  const onSearch = vi.fn();
  const user = userEvent.setup({ advanceTimers: vi.advanceTimersByTime });
  render(<SearchBox onSearch={onSearch} />);
  const box = screen.getByRole('searchbox', { name: /search/i });

  await user.type(box, 'ab');

  // Negative edge: below the threshold nothing happened.
  vi.advanceTimersByTime(DEBOUNCE_MS - 1);
  expect(onSearch).not.toHaveBeenCalled();

  // Positive edge: crossing it produces exactly one call, with the final query.
  vi.advanceTimersByTime(1);
  expect(onSearch).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);
  expect(onSearch).toHaveBeenCalledWith('ab');
});

it('restarts the window while the user keeps typing', async () => {
  const onSearch = vi.fn();
  const user = userEvent.setup({ advanceTimers: vi.advanceTimersByTime });
  render(<SearchBox onSearch={onSearch} />);
  const box = screen.getByRole('searchbox', { name: /search/i });

  await user.type(box, 'ab');
  vi.advanceTimersByTime(DEBOUNCE_MS - 100); // still inside the window
  await user.type(box, 'c');                 // resets the debounce

  vi.advanceTimersByTime(DEBOUNCE_MS - 1);
  expect(onSearch).not.toHaveBeenCalled();

  vi.advanceTimersByTime(1);
  expect(onSearch).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);
  expect(onSearch).toHaveBeenCalledWith('abc');
});
```

`DEBOUNCE_MS - 1` is what makes the test falsifiable: an implementation with no debounce calls immediately and fails the first assertion; an implementation that fires twice per burst fails the count; one that keeps a stale query fails the argument check, which the original `toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1)` alone would have missed.

**If the debounced work is asynchronous** — it fetches, or the parent rerenders from the callback — advancing the clock is only half of it. The timer settles synchronously, the promise continuation does not, so assert the observable result rather than the timer: `vi.advanceTimersByTime(DEBOUNCE_MS); await screen.findByRole('status')` (or `await vi.waitFor(...)` in Vitest, which advances fake timers by its interval while polling). What must never appear is `await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 300))` or an arbitrary `waitFor` with a real timeout — those reintroduce the wall clock the fake timers exist to remove.

**Boundaries.** Prefer `advanceTimersByTime` over `runAllTimers` whenever anything reschedules itself (a poll after a search, a retry loop): `runAllTimers` drains recursively and aborts on a self-rearming queue. And keep the assertion on the contract — the query string the component hands to `onSearch` — not on the fact that `setTimeout` was called with `300`.
